  "risks": [
    {
      "risk": "A random code collision or concurrent creation could cause a duplicate-code failure or overwrite an existing link.",
      "mitigation": "Use a database unique constraint, detect duplicate insert failures, and retry generation within a bounded number of attempts before returning an internal error."
    },
    {
      "risk": "A read-modify-write click update can lose increments under concurrent follow requests.",
      "mitigation": "Serialize follow loads with a pessimistic row lock in a transaction or use an atomic SQL increment, and cover concurrent requests with an integration test."
    },
    {
      "risk": "URI parsing may accept relative URLs, unsupported schemes, or hostless values if validation only checks parse success.",
      "mitigation": "Require an absolute URI, scheme exactly equal to http or https, and a nonblank host before persistence."
    },
    {
      "risk": "Short URL generation can produce incorrect host, port, or scheme values when no configured base URL is supplied.",
      "mitigation": "Centralize construction, prefer shortener.base-url when configured, and test request-derived URLs with representative schemes and ports."
    },
    {
      "risk": "Spring's default exception handling could return HTML or an inconsistent error shape for malformed JSON or missing fields.",
      "mitigation": "Register a controller advice covering validation, deserialization, invalid URL, and not-found exceptions and always emit the stable JSON error schema."
    },
    {
      "risk": "The broad follow route could conflict with API routes or allow reserved names to be treated as codes.",
      "mitigation": "Keep API mappings at multiple path segments, reserve api during code generation, and verify unknown API and follow paths in integration tests."
    }
  ],
  "decisions": [
    {
      "title": "Use a fixed seven-character cryptographically random code",
      "rationale": "The specification requires exactly seven URL-safe characters that are difficult to guess; a secure random alphanumeric generator satisfies this without exposing sequential identifiers.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Use database-generated numeric IDs encoded into short strings",
        "Use deterministic hashing of the original URL"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use a database uniqueness constraint plus collision retry",
      "rationale": "Checking for an unused code before insert is vulnerable to races; database enforcement is authoritative and retrying preserves the required no-overwrite behavior.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Use an in-memory set of issued codes",
        "Fail creation immediately on any collision"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Protect follow increments with transactional row locking or an atomic update",
      "rationale": "Every successful follow must be counted exactly once, including concurrent requests; ordinary entity read-modify-write is insufficient.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Use an optimistic version field and retry transactions",
        "Perform an unprotected in-memory increment"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Represent invalid input and missing links with error and message fields",
      "rationale": "The approved requirements define stable identifiers invalid_url and not_found and require human-readable JSON errors.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Expose framework-default Problem Details responses",
        "Return plain text error messages"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Configure shortener.base-url with request-derived fallback",
      "rationale": "This follows the approved assumption while allowing deployments to advertise a public URL independent of the inbound request host.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Require shortener.base-url in every environment",
        "Always derive the URL from the request"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Keep duplicate original URLs as separate links",
      "rationale": "The requirements explicitly allow repeated submissions to create independent codes and do not require deduplication.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Return an existing link for the same original URL",
        "Enforce uniqueness on the original URL"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the existing configured H2 database without restart persistence requirements",
      "rationale": "Spring Data JPA and the configured H2 database are required, while persistence across application restarts is explicitly not required.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Add a separate external database configuration",
        "Store links only in memory"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "designDoc": "# URL Shortener Design\n\n## Scope and architecture\n\nThis is a greenfield Spring Boot service in package `com.example.shortener`, using the existing application class, configured H2 database, Spring Data JPA, Bean Validation, and constructor injection.\n\nLayers:\n\n- `LinkController`: HTTP mappings, request binding, validation boundary, response status and redirect construction.\n- `LinkService`: transactional creation, metadata lookup, and follow operations.\n- `LinkRepository`: persistence, unique-code lookup, and atomic click-count update.\n- `Link`: JPA entity containing the persisted link state.\n- `CodeGenerator`: cryptographically secure seven-character code generation.\n- `UrlValidator`: absolute HTTP(S) URL validation.\n- `ShortUrlBuilder`: configured-base or request-derived short URL construction.\n- `ApiExceptionHandler`: converts validation, malformed JSON, not-found, and internal collision-exhaustion errors to JSON RFC 7807 `ProblemDetail` responses.\n\nNo external infrastructure is required. H2 remains in-memory and `ddl-auto: update` creates the schema during application startup. Data persistence across restarts is intentionally not provided.\n\n## Data model\n\n`Link` is mapped to table `links`:\n\n- `id`: generated `Long` primary key; internal only.\n- `code`: non-null, exactly seven characters, unique database constraint, indexed through the unique constraint.\n- `originalUrl`: non-null URL string; use a column length sufficient for normal HTTP URLs, such as 2048 characters.\n- `clickCount`: non-null `long`, initialized to zero.\n\nThe database uniqueness constraint is authoritative. Application-side existence checks are not sufficient because concurrent requests can generate the same code.\n\nThe response representation is not persisted; `shortUrl` is constructed from the configured base URL or the current request.\n\n## Request and response records\n\nUse Java records:\n\n- `CreateLinkRequest(String url)` with `@NotBlank` on `url`.\n- `LinkResponse(String code, String shortUrl, String originalUrl, long clickCount)`.\n- `ApiError` may be represented through `ProblemDetail`, with properties `error` and `message`.\n\nA valid create request still undergoes semantic URL validation after Bean Validation. Missing, null, blank, malformed, relative, hostless, and unsupported-scheme values all map to the stable error identifier `invalid_url`.\n\n## URL validation\n\nParse the supplied value as `java.net.URI`. Reject parsing failures and require:\n\n- `isAbsolute()` to be true.\n- Scheme equal to `http` or `https`, case-insensitively.\n- A nonblank host from `URI.getHost()`.\n\nQuery strings, fragments, user-info, and nonstandard ports are allowed when URI parsing and host validation succeed. No DNS lookup or destination availability check is performed. The original submitted string is persisted and returned unchanged.\n\n## Code generation and collision handling\n\n`CodeGenerator` uses `SecureRandom` and a URL-safe alphanumeric alphabet, for example `A-Z`, `a-z`, and `0-9`. It generates exactly seven characters. The reserved code `api` is not possible because generated codes always have seven characters; the generator may nevertheless support a reserved-code check for defense in depth.\n\nCreation attempts are bounded, for example to 10 attempts:\n\n1. Generate a new seven-character code.\n2. Construct a new `Link` with click count zero.\n3. Insert it through JPA.\n4. If the insert fails because of the unique-code constraint, discard that entity and retry with a new code.\n5. After the bounded retry limit, return HTTP 500 with a stable internal error response rather than overwriting an existing link.\n\nThe retrying public service method delegates each insert attempt to a separate transactional method or otherwise clears the failed persistence context, preventing a failed transaction from poisoning later attempts.\n\nRepeated submissions of the same original URL never deduplicate and normally produce different codes.\n\n## Short URL construction\n\nThe `shortener.base-url` property is optional.\n\n- If configured, trim trailing slashes and append `/{code}`.\n- If absent, derive the base from the current request's scheme, host, and effective port, then append `/{code}`.\n- Do not include a default port in the derived result when it is the standard port for the request scheme.\n\nThe construction logic is centralized in `ShortUrlBuilder` and used by creation and metadata responses. A request context is therefore required for the response-building controller paths when no configured base exists.\n\n## Request flows\n\n### Create\n\n`POST /api/links` binds JSON to `CreateLinkRequest`, applies Bean Validation, validates the URI, and calls `LinkService.create`. The service persists a new link with count zero and returns `LinkResponse`. The controller returns HTTP 201 and JSON.\n\n### Metadata lookup\n\n`GET /api/links/{code}` calls a read-only service operation. It loads by exact code and does not update the entity or count. A missing code raises `LinkNotFoundException`, mapped to HTTP 404.\n\n### Follow\n\n`GET /{code}` rejects the reserved route value `api` as not found, then calls the follow service operation. The service obtains the link's destination and performs an atomic database increment. The controller returns HTTP 302 Found with a `Location` header containing the original URL and no JSON success body.\n\nA successful follow increments the count exactly once. Metadata reads never increment it.\n\n## Concurrency and reliability\n\nThe follow operation must not use a detached read-modify-write sequence. Use a JPQL bulk update such as `update Link l set l.clickCount = l.clickCount + 1 where l.code = :code`, executed in a transaction. First load the link to obtain the destination, then execute the atomic update and require one affected row. Since links are not deleted, the row remains valid between the read and update. Every successful follow request contributes one database increment, including concurrent requests.\n\nAn alternative is a pessimistic row lock followed by entity mutation, but the atomic update has shorter lock duration and avoids lost updates.\n\nCreation uses the database unique constraint plus bounded retries. Unknown codes do not insert or update anything. H2 transaction and JPA exceptions are translated at the service/controller boundary rather than exposed as implementation details.\n\n## Error handling\n\n`ApiExceptionHandler` returns RFC 7807 JSON `ProblemDetail` responses with properties:\n\n- `error`: stable machine-readable identifier.\n- `message`: non-empty human-readable explanation.\n\nThe response content type is `application/problem+json` where negotiated; it remains JSON and is compatible with clients checking the required fields.\n\nMappings:\n\n- Missing, blank, malformed JSON field, Bean Validation failure, or invalid URL: HTTP 400, `error: invalid_url`.\n- Unknown code: HTTP 404, `error: not_found`.\n- Exhausted code-collision retries or unexpected persistence failure: HTTP 500 with an internal stable error identifier and no database details.\n\nMalformed JSON and missing request bodies are handled explicitly so Spring's default HTML or inconsistent error representation is not exposed.\n\n## Backward compatibility\n\nThis is greenfield, so no existing endpoint or persisted schema contract must be preserved. The existing `UrlShortenerApplication` remains unchanged. The design uses only dependencies already listed in the project and `jakarta.*` APIs. It does not require build changes or additional infrastructure.\n\n## Testing requirements\n\nUse JUnit 5, AssertJ, Mockito, and Spring Boot HTTP integration tests with `@SpringBootTest` and `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` from `org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureMockMvc`. Verify creation, duplicate submissions, all invalid URL classes, metadata non-mutation, redirect `Location`, unknown-code errors, seven-character codes, configured and request-derived short URLs, and concurrent follows. Do not use `@DataJpaTest`, `TestRestTemplate`, or `WebTestClient`.\n",
  "apiContract": [
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "path": "/api/links",
      "request": {
        "contentType": "application/json",
        "json": {
          "url": "https://example.com/path"
        }
      },
      "responses": [
        {
          "status": 201,
          "body": {
            "code": "Ab3xYz9",
            "shortUrl": "http://localhost:8080/Ab3xYz9",
            "originalUrl": "https://example.com/path",
            "clickCount": 0
          }
        }
      ],
      "errors": [
        {
          "status": 400,
          "when": "The body is missing, malformed, missing url, contains a null or blank url, or url is not an absolute HTTP(S) URL with a valid host. ProblemDetail properties include error=invalid_url and a non-empty message."
        },
        {
          "status": 500,
          "when": "A unique code cannot be generated after the bounded collision retry limit or an unexpected persistence failure occurs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "method": "GET",
      "path": "/api/links/{code}",
      "request": null,
      "responses": [
        {
          "status": 200,
          "body": {
            "code": "Ab3xYz9",
            "shortUrl": "http://localhost:8080/Ab3xYz9",
            "originalUrl": "https://example.com/path",
            "clickCount": 3
          }
        }
      ],
      "errors": [
        {
          "status": 404,
          "when": "No persisted link has the requested code. ProblemDetail properties include error=not_found and a non-empty message."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "method": "GET",
      "path": "/{code}",
      "request": null,
      "responses": [
        {
          "status": 302,
          "body": null,
          "headers": {
            "Location": "https://example.com/path"
          }
        }
      ],
      "errors": [
        {
          "status": 404,
          "when": "No persisted link has the requested code, including the reserved or API route value api. ProblemDetail properties include error=not_found and a non-empty message."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "decisions": [
    {
      "title": "Use seven-character cryptographically random alphanumeric codes",
      "rationale": "This satisfies the fixed-length, URL-safe, and hard-to-guess requirements while remaining simple to implement with Java SecureRandom.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Sequential numeric IDs were rejected because they are guessable.",
        "Hashing the original URL was rejected because repeated submissions must be allowed to create separate links and hashes can expose URL relationships.",
        "UUID strings were rejected because they are substantially longer than required."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Enforce uniqueness in both application retry logic and the database",
      "rationale": "The database constraint prevents overwrites during concurrent creation, while bounded retry logic turns random collisions into successful new links.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Checking existence before insert alone was rejected because two concurrent requests can pass the check.",
        "Unbounded retries were rejected because a persistent database problem could hang requests."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use an atomic JPQL click-count update",
      "rationale": "An atomic increment guarantees that concurrent successful follows do not lose updates and avoids keeping a row lock for a longer read-modify-write transaction.",
      "alternatives": [
        "普通 entity read-modify-write was rejected because concurrent requests can overwrite each other's increments.",
        "Pessimistic row locking was rejected as the primary approach because it holds database locks longer, although it would also be correct."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Return HTTP 302 Found for successful follows",
      "rationale": "It is an explicit, broadly supported temporary redirect and meets the requirement for a 3xx response with a Location header.",
      "alternatives": [
        "301 permanent redirect was rejected because links and service behavior should not be cached permanently by clients.",
        "307 temporary redirect was rejected because the requested behavior is a simple GET navigation and 302 is the conventional shortener response."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Construct shortUrl from an optional configured base URL, otherwise from the request",
      "rationale": "This supports deployment behind different hosts while allowing a stable public URL to be configured through `shortener.base-url`.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Hardcoding localhost was rejected because it is incorrect outside local development.",
        "Requiring the property was rejected because the stated assumption requires a useful request-derived fallback."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use RFC 7807 ProblemDetail with error and message properties",
      "rationale": "ProblemDetail provides the requested standards-based JSON error format while stable custom properties preserve the required client contract.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Returning ad hoc error records was rejected because it would not provide the standard ProblemDetail shape.",
        "Using Spring's default error responses was rejected because malformed requests could produce inconsistent or non-JSON output."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Persist only the original URL, code, and aggregate count",
      "rationale": "These are the required link details and click aggregate; no out-of-scope analytics or history is needed.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Persisting click events was rejected because click history and timestamps are explicitly out of scope.",
        "Persisting shortUrl was rejected because it depends on deployment configuration or the incoming request."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Reserve API routing explicitly",
      "rationale": "The one-segment follow mapping must not accidentally treat API route names as short codes, and generated codes must remain independent from API paths.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Relying only on Spring mapping precedence was rejected because `/api` can still match the one-segment route.",
        "Changing the follow path prefix was rejected because the required public follow endpoint is `/{code}`."
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{"files":[{"path":"src/main/java/com/example/shortener/Link.java","content":"package com.example.shortener;

import jakarta.persistence.Column;
import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.GeneratedValue;
import jakarta.persistence.GenerationType;
import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import jakarta.persistence.Table;

@Entity
@Table(name = "links")
public class Link {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true, length = 7)
    private String code;

    @Column(name = "original_url", nullable = false, length = 2048)
    private String originalUrl;

    @Column(name = "click_count", nullable = false)
    private long clickCount;

    protected Link() {
    }

    public Link(String code, String originalUrl) {
        this.code = code;
        this.originalUrl = originalUrl;
        this.clickCount = 0;
    }

    public String getCode() {
        return code;
    }

    public String getOriginalUrl() {
        return originalUrl;
    }

    public long getClickCount() {
        return clickCount;
    }
}
